WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.377
Every good report needs a calendar table.

2
00:00:03.377 --> 00:00:08.530
In this lesson, you will learn to build one with a single DAX formula,

3
00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:14.788
set the start and end dates with DATE and NOW, and see the date hierarchy appear automatically.

4
00:00:14.788 --> 00:00:16.260
Let's build our calendar!

5
00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.909
We are on the Date and Time page.

6
00:00:20.909 --> 00:00:23.279
On the Modeling tab, click New table.

7
00:00:23.279 --> 00:00:27.680
A table can be created with DAX, not just loaded from a file.

8
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.766
I type CALENDAR. The tooltip says:

9
00:00:37.766 --> 00:00:43.620
returns a table with one column of all dates between a start date and an end date.

10
00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:58.942
My first try:

11
00:00:58.942 --> 00:01:03.081
CALENDAR, from January first, two thousand twenty five, to NOW.

12
00:01:03.081 --> 00:01:07.220
NOW gives today's date, so the calendar always reaches today.

13
00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:34.289
Press Enter, and the new table, Calendar two, appears in the Data pane, with one column: Date.

14
00:01:34.289 --> 00:01:37.002
Power BI also builds a date hierarchy:

15
00:01:37.002 --> 00:01:38.940
Year, Quarter, Month, and Day.

16
00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:44.020
I show the dates in a table visual, so we can see every day listed.

17
00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:16.849
Text dates can be risky, so I use the DATE function instead:

18
00:02:16.849 --> 00:02:19.269
DATE, two thousand twenty five, one, one.

19
00:02:19.269 --> 00:02:22.380
DATE takes the year, the month, and the day.

20
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.365
For a fixed range, use DATE for both ends.

21
00:02:51.365 --> 00:02:57.880
Here, a second calendar runs from January first to January thirty first, two thousand twenty five.

22
00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:25.985
The new table appears with its own date hierarchy.

23
00:03:25.985 --> 00:03:30.000
One line of DAX, and you have a full calendar. Fantastic!

